WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.740
TCP services often need concurrency, but concurrency should be a design choice rather than an accident.

2
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:14.520
Thread-per-connection, worker pools, event loops, nonblocking I/O, and hybrid models all have different tradeoffs.

3
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:20.060
The right model is one the team can bound, test, observe, and maintain.

4
00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.540
Per-client state should be isolated where possible.

5
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:31.800
Input buffers, parser state, authentication state, pending output, timeout state, and cleanup ownership should not bleed across clients.

6
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:39.900
Isolation makes it easier to reason about one connection and reduces the chance that one client's behavior affects another client's state.

7
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.660
Shared mutable data needs synchronization and clear ownership.

8
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:55.240
Configuration snapshots, counters, caches, session tables, authorization data, rate state, and global shutdown flags should be protected according to the concurrency model.

9
00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:03.900
Reviewers should look for unsynchronized reads, inconsistent lock ordering, and state transitions that cross thread boundaries without a clear contract.

10
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.220
Bounded concurrency is part of resource control.

11
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.640
A worker pool should have a maximum size.

12
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.100
An event loop should have bounded queues and buffers.

13
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.680
A thread-per-connection design should have an explicit connection cap and cleanup plan.

14
00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:23.300
Without bounds, concurrency can turn normal traffic variation into unpredictable resource use.

15
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:28.940
Cleanup ownership is especially important when work moves between threads or callbacks.

16
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:39.120
The design should define who closes the descriptor, who frees buffers, who records final state, and what happens if shutdown begins while work is in progress.

17
00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:45.620
Tests should exercise cancellation, disconnects, worker failure, and normal completion.
